REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w03","title":"Clauses, complex sentences, complements and adjuncts","summary":"How English sentences are built from clauses (main and subordinate; noun, adjectival and adverbial; simple, compound and complex sentences), and which parts a sentence must have (complements) and which it can drop (adjuncts).","n":3,"lectures":[{"code":"3.1","title":"Sentence, clause, and complex sentences","video":"iO55So_lamA","seconds":1815},{"code":"3.2","title":"Describing clauses & sentences | mastering independent & subordinate clauses","video":"oJP60we1YUY","seconds":2528},{"code":"3.3","title":"Illustrating clauses & sentences | understanding embedded, simple, compound & complex forms","video":"Rse0Rxh1SlM","seconds":2154},{"code":"3.4","title":"Adjectival (relative)clause | modifying nouns in complex and compound sentences","video":"EK2zwv9WO1A","seconds":1706},{"code":"3.5","title":"Required & optional elements in english sentences (complements & adjuncts)","video":"3nAOsJgIuDo","seconds":2586}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"building-blocks-of-a-sentence","level":2,"title":"Building blocks of a sentence"},{"id":"complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause","level":2,"title":"Complex sentences and the kinds of clause"},{"id":"a-long-sentence-taken-apart","level":3,"title":"A long sentence, taken apart"},{"id":"three-kinds-of-subordinate-clause","level":3,"title":"Three kinds of subordinate clause"},{"id":"the-three-jobs-of-a-noun-clause","level":3,"title":"The three jobs of a noun clause"},{"id":"clause-analysis-in-three-steps","level":3,"title":"Clause analysis in three steps"},{"id":"sentence-and-clause-finite-and-non-finite","level":2,"title":"Sentence and clause: finite and non-finite"},{"id":"finite-and-non-finite-clauses","level":3,"title":"Finite and non-finite clauses"},{"id":"what-a-clause-can-do-in-a-sentence","level":3,"title":"What a clause can do in a sentence"},{"id":"simple-compound-and-complex-in-real-texts","level":2,"title":"Simple, compound and complex in real texts"},{"id":"a-speech-and-a-novel","level":3,"title":"A speech and a novel"},{"id":"independent-clauses-and-simple-sentences","level":3,"title":"Independent clauses and simple sentences"},{"id":"compound-sentences","level":3,"title":"Compound sentences"},{"id":"the-adjectival-relative-clause","level":2,"title":"The adjectival (relative) clause"},{"id":"one-main-clause-two-different-subordinate-clauses","level":3,"title":"One main clause, two different subordinate clauses"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-activity","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s activity"},{"id":"complements-and-adjuncts","level":2,"title":"Complements and adjuncts"},{"id":"word-order-and-agreement","level":3,"title":"Word order and agreement"},{"id":"imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject","level":3,"title":"Imperatives have a hidden subject"},{"id":"testing-for-complements-take-it-out","level":3,"title":"Testing for complements: take it out"},{"id":"testing-for-adjuncts-take-it-out","level":3,"title":"Testing for adjuncts: take it out"},{"id":"an-adjunct-of-the-verb-or-of-a-noun","level":3,"title":"An adjunct of the verb or of a noun?"},{"id":"complements-and-adjuncts-inside-a-phrase","level":3,"title":"Complements and adjuncts inside a phrase"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>clause</strong> is a group of words with its own subject and its own verb with tense. A sentence that stands on its own is an <strong>independent clause</strong> (also called the <strong>main</strong> or <strong>matrix</strong> clause). A clause placed inside another one is a <strong>subordinate clause</strong> (also called an <strong>embedded</strong> clause).</li>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> sentence: one independent clause. <strong>Compound</strong> sentence: two or more independent clauses joined by <em>and</em>, <em>or</em>, <em>but</em> or commas. <strong>Complex</strong> sentence: an independent clause with one or more subordinate clauses.</li>\n<li>A subordinate clause does one of three jobs. A <strong>noun clause</strong> is a subject, an object or a clause in apposition; an <strong>adjectival clause</strong> describes a noun; an <strong>adverbial clause</strong> adds extra information.</li>\n<li><strong>Clause analysis</strong> has three steps: count the clauses, name their <strong>nature</strong> (noun, adjectival, adverbial) and state their <strong>function</strong> (what each one does, and to which word).</li>\n<li>A clause is <strong>finite</strong> when its verb has tense (<em>Sarita loves flowers</em>) and <strong>non-finite</strong> when it does not (<em>to eat pizza</em>). Only a finite clause can also stand alone as a sentence.</li>\n<li>The <strong>adjectival (relative) clause</strong> starts with a word such as <em>who</em>, <em>which</em> or <em>that</em>, comes <strong>after</strong> the noun it describes (an adjective comes before), and can be replaced by one adjective.</li>\n<li>A <strong>complement</strong> is a required part, such as the object of a verb: remove it and the sentence breaks. An <strong>adjunct</strong> is optional, such as <em>in the morning</em>: remove it and the sentence is still complete.</li>\n<li>A complement stays next to its head word, and a phrase has only one (a sentence at most two). Adjuncts stand further out, and there can be many.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"building-blocks-of-a-sentence\">Building blocks of a sentence</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Clauses are built from the same parts as plain sentences, so we start there. Take the lecture’s first example, <em>Raju likes flowers</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). It names someone (<em>Raju</em>) and then says something about him (<em>likes flowers</em>). Every English sentence has these two parts. Once you can find them, the lectures promise, you can see through even a very long sentence “like an X-ray machine”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence, subject and predicate</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sentence</strong> = subject + predicate (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The <strong>subject</strong> is who or what the sentence is about: <em>Raju</em>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>predicate</strong> is the rest of the sentence; it says something about the subject: <em>likes flowers</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Predicate</strong> = verb + object(s) + adjuncts (adverbs). The <strong>verb</strong> is the most important part: every other part of the predicate is there because of the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Verb</td>\n<td>the action or state word</td>\n<td><em>likes</em> in <em>Raju likes flowers</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tense</td>\n<td>the time form of a verb</td>\n<td><em>loves</em> (present), <em>loved</em> (past)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object</td>\n<td>the person or thing the verb acts on; it answers “likes <strong>what</strong>?”</td>\n<td><em>flowers</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transitive verb</td>\n<td>a verb that needs an object</td>\n<td><em>like, love, know, need</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intransitive verb</td>\n<td>a verb that takes no object</td>\n<td><em>come, sit down, disappear</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreement</td>\n<td>the verb’s form matches the subject’s number (one or many) and person (<em>I</em>, <em>you</em>, <em>he/she</em>)</td>\n<td><em>Raju likes</em>, but <em>John and Mary like</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Phrase and clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>clause</strong> has its own subject and a verb with tense, often with a helping verb (an <strong>auxiliary</strong>, such as <em>was</em>, <em>had</em>, <em>would</em>): <em>which <strong>was</strong> only a corner</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>phrase</strong> is a group of words that belong together but have no subject and no verb with tense: <em>on the roof</em>, <em>a glass of water</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> (PP) starts with a preposition (<em>in, on, at, with, before, after, by, of, for</em>): <em>in the evening</em>. A <strong>noun phrase</strong> (NP) is a noun with its small words: <em>a glass of water</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture’s test: “we are only talking about a clause when it has some verb in it, some auxiliary in it, some tense in it, subject, etc.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Complex sentences and the kinds of clause</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some sentences carry another sentence inside them. <em>He still believed that his Albert Mission set was intact</em> contains the sentence <em>his Albert Mission set was intact</em>. The outer sentence is the <strong>main clause</strong>; the inner one is a <strong>subordinate clause</strong>, because it depends on (hangs on) a word of the main clause. Such sentences are normal, not special: “we often use as many or even more complex sentences than simple or compound ones” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). The lecturer adds that there is no shortcut: we learn this only by taking real sentences apart.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Independent clause and subordinate clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>independent clause</strong> is a sentence with independent status: it has a subject and a predicate and does not depend on another clause. Other names: <strong>main clause</strong>, <strong>matrix clause</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>subordinate clause</strong> depends on some part of the main clause and cannot stand as the sentence by itself. Other names: <strong>embedded clause</strong>, <strong>dependent clause</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compound sentence and complex sentence</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>compound sentence</strong> is two or more sentences connected by a comma or by a joining word (a <strong>conjunction</strong>) such as <em>and</em> or <em>or</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>complex sentence</strong> has an independent clause and <strong>one or more</strong> subordinate clauses.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-long-sentence-taken-apart\">A long sentence, taken apart</h3>\n<p>The lecture reads a paragraph from R. K. Narayan’s novel <em>Swami and Friends</em> and lists seven complex sentences from it, a to g (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>). Sentence a has only one full stop:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>After making his exit from Albert Mission School in that theatrical manner (on the day following the strike), Swaminathan became so consistently stubborn that a few days later his father took him to the Board School and admitted him there.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence a, clause by clause (the lecture’s table at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find the verbs with tense: <em>became</em>, <em>took</em>, <em>admitted</em>. Three such verbs, so three clauses.</li>\n<li>Main (independent) clause: <strong>Swaminathan became so consistently stubborn</strong>. Subject <em>Swaminathan</em>, verb <em>became</em>, then the adjective phrase <em>so consistently stubborn</em>.</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause 1: <strong>that a few days later his father took him to the Board School</strong>.</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause 2: <strong>and (his father) admitted him there</strong>. The subject <em>his father</em> is not repeated, so the slide adds it in brackets.</li>\n<li>The opening words <em>After making his exit … (on the day following the strike)</em> set the scene in time. The lecturer brackets them as one block but does not analyse them further.</li>\n<li>Function, from the slide: both subordinate clauses <strong>complement the adjective phrase “so consistently stubborn”</strong>. They say <em>how</em> stubborn: so stubborn <em>that</em> his father moved him to another school.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one main clause and two subordinate clauses. The two subordinate clauses are joined to each other by <em>and</em>, so “within the subordinate clause, you have a compound sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>); the sentence as a whole is complex.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>More sentences from the list, as the lecture analyses them. Square brackets mark where each subordinate clause begins and ends.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n<th>Subordinate clause and its job</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>b. But he excited the curiosity [that all newcomers do], and found himself … the centre of attraction in Second C.</td>\n<td>he excited the curiosity</td>\n<td><em>that all newcomers do</em>: adjectival; it qualifies the noun <em>curiosity</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>c. He had not yet picked the few [that he would have liked to call his chums].</td>\n<td>He had not yet picked the few</td>\n<td><em>that he would have liked to call his chums</em>: it modifies <em>the few</em>, the object of <em>picked</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>d. He still believed [that his Albert Mission set was intact], though, since the reopening in June, the set was not [what it had been before].</td>\n<td>He still believed</td>\n<td><em>that his Albert Mission set was intact</em>: noun clause, object of <em>believed</em>. <em>what it had been before</em>: noun clause in apposition to the noun <em>set</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>e. Sankar disappeared, and people said [that his father had been transferred]; …</td>\n<td>Sankar disappeared / people said</td>\n<td><em>that his father had been transferred</em>: object of <em>said</em> (the same pattern as d)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Sentence e goes on with semicolons (;) and more clauses, and “all put together makes one sentence” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). Sentences f and g are read out but not analysed; g is a check question below.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">One sentence, several subordinate clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1030s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:10</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>One subordinate clause can differ from another “not only in length, but also in its function”.</li>\n<li>The two subordinate clauses in sentence a are alike: both complement <em>so consistently stubborn</em>.</li>\n<li>But one sentence can also have two or three subordinate clauses doing two or three different jobs.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"three-kinds-of-subordinate-clause\">Three kinds of subordinate clause</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun, adjectival and adverbial clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Ordinarily speaking there are three kinds of subordinate clauses”, named after the job they do:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>noun clause</strong> (nominal clause) works like a noun;</li>\n<li>an <strong>adjectival clause</strong> works like an adjective;</li>\n<li>an <strong>adverbial clause</strong> works like an adverb.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Its job</th>\n<th>Example (clause in bold)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Noun</strong> clause: works like a noun</td>\n<td>subject or object of a verb, or apposition</td>\n<td>He still believed <strong>that his Albert Mission set was intact</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adjectival</strong> clause: works like an adjective</td>\n<td>qualifies (describes) a noun</td>\n<td>He excited the curiosity <strong>that all newcomers do</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Adverbial</strong> clause: works like an adverb</td>\n<td>gives additional information</td>\n<td><strong>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums</strong>, Swaminathan was gazing on his face. (Lecture 3.4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">“He excited the curiosity that all newcomers do” (the lecture’s table at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:40</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Independent clause: <strong>he excited the curiosity</strong>. Subject <em>he</em>, verb <em>excited</em>, object <em>the curiosity</em>.</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause: <strong>that all newcomers do</strong>.</li>\n<li>Which word does it hang on? It tells us <em>which</em> curiosity: the kind that all newcomers excite. So it describes the noun <em>curiosity</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the subordinate clause works like an adjective and qualifies <em>curiosity</em> in the independent clause.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-jobs-of-a-noun-clause\">The three jobs of a noun clause</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun clause: subject, object or apposition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A noun clause can be</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the <strong>subject</strong> of a verb in the independent clause;</li>\n<li>the <strong>object</strong> of a verb in the independent clause;</li>\n<li>a clause <strong>in apposition</strong>: it gives additional information about a noun in the independent clause, but it cannot stand by itself. (<strong>Apposition</strong> means putting something right next to a noun to say more about the same thing.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Lecture sentence (noun clause in bold)</th>\n<th>How you know</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject</td>\n<td><strong>That the pandemic would disappear</strong> was believed last year by everyone.</td>\n<td>It sits in the subject place, before <em>was believed</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object</td>\n<td>He still believed <strong>that his Albert Mission set was intact</strong>.</td>\n<td><em>believed</em> is transitive: believed <strong>what</strong>? The clause.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apposition</td>\n<td>… the set was not <strong>what it had been before</strong>.</td>\n<td>It adds information about the noun <em>set</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecturer first says the pandemic sentence as <em>It was believed last year by everyone that the pandemic would disappear</em>. There <em>it</em> only holds the place of the clause. Put the clause in that place and it becomes the subject of this <strong>passive</strong> sentence (a sentence whose subject receives the action: <em>was believed</em>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rabindranath Tagore (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1445s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:05</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>Rabindranath Tagore, who is a Nobel Laureate, was not only a great writer, but also a great manager. He ran a school, a university, a printing press, and a journal nearly all his life which is a life worth talking about.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>The paragraph has two sentences. The slide shows that sentence 1 has three clauses (missing words in brackets):\n<ul>\n<li>Rabindranath Tagore was not only a great writer</li>\n<li>(Rabindranath Tagore) was also a Nobel Laureate</li>\n<li>(Rabindranath Tagore) but (was) also a great manager</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The second clause appears as <em>who is a Nobel Laureate</em>, right after the noun <em>Rabindranath Tagore</em>, between commas.</li>\n<li>The slide says it is not an adjectival clause but “a subordinate noun clause in apposition. It does not qualify the preceding noun, it provides additional information about the main noun.”</li>\n<li>The lecturer adds that such a clause is also called a <strong>relative clause</strong> (a clause that begins with <em>who</em>, <em>which</em> or <em>that</em> and refers back to a noun), and that it gives “additional information in the form of modification” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> three clauses. Two say what Tagore was (a great writer, a great manager); the clause in apposition, <em>who is a Nobel Laureate</em>, only adds a fact about him.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Apposition: noun clause or adjectival clause?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lectures use two labels for a <em>who</em>-clause between commas. The 3.1 slide calls <em>who is a Nobel Laureate</em> a <strong>noun clause in apposition</strong>, but the lecturer then calls it a relative clause and writes “Adjectival” over the box (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1580s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:20</a>). Lectures 3.2 and 3.4 call the same pattern (<em>Raju [who lives in Chennai]</em>, <em>D. Pillai, who had earned a name …</em>) an <strong>adjectival (relative) clause</strong>. The key point is the same in both: the clause <strong>does not pick out which person</strong>; it only <strong>adds information</strong> about a noun that is already clear.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"clause-analysis-in-three-steps\">Clause analysis in three steps</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Clause analysis (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1275s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:15</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Identify</strong> the number of clauses.</li>\n<li>Understand their <strong>nature</strong>: noun, adjectival or adverbial clause.</li>\n<li>Understand their <strong>function</strong>: “x qualifies y” (adjectival clause), or it is the subject or object of something (noun clause), or it adds information (adverbial clause).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">One verb with tense = one clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Underline every verb that shows tense (<em>became, took, admitted</em>): that count is the number of clauses. Then ask of each subordinate clause: <strong>which word does it hang on, and which question does it answer?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Believed <strong>what</strong>?” → noun clause (object).</li>\n<li>“<strong>Which</strong> curiosity?” → adjectival clause.</li>\n<li>“<strong>When</strong>? <strong>About what</strong>?” → adverbial clause.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s exercise: find the main clause and the noun clause in <em>Rajam realized at this point that the starting of a cricket team was the most complicated problem on earth.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Verbs with tense: <em>realized</em>, <em>was</em>. So two clauses.</li>\n<li>Main clause: <strong>Rajam realized at this point</strong>.</li>\n<li>Realized <strong>what</strong>? → <strong>that the starting of a cricket team was the most complicated problem on earth</strong>. It answers “what?”, so it is a <strong>noun clause</strong>, the object of <em>realized</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the lecture’s answer slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:10</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find the main clause and the noun clause in <em>The Government did not seem to know where it ought to interfere and where not.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Main clause: <strong>The Government did not seem to know</strong>.</li>\n<li>Know <strong>what</strong>? → <strong>where it ought to interfere and where not</strong>: a noun clause, the object of <em>to know</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here <em>where</em> does not ask a question; it starts the clause. (The lecture’s answer slide, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iO55So_lamA&amp;t=1750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:10</a>.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What job does the bold clause do? <em><strong>That the pandemic would disappear</strong> was believed last year by everyone.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>It is a <strong>noun clause</strong> working as the <strong>subject</strong> of <em>was believed</em>. It stands where a subject stands (before the verb), and <em>it</em> can take its place: <em>It was believed last year by everyone that …</em></p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Sentence g on the lecture’s list is <em>But Rajam had not spoken to him since the day when his political doings became known.</em> Which noun does <em>when his political doings became known</em> describe, and what kind of clause is it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>It describes <strong>the day</strong>: which day? The day when his political doings became known. So it is an <strong>adjectival</strong> clause. It comes right after its noun, and <em>when</em> here is not a question word but starts the clause (the rule of Lecture 3.4). The lecture leaves this sentence for you; this answer applies its rules.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"sentence-and-clause-finite-and-non-finite\">Sentence and clause: finite and non-finite</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 3.2 asks: what is the difference between a sentence and a clause? It answers with four sentences about Raju (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). All four are good sentences: each has the subject <em>Raju</em>, a transitive verb that agrees with <em>Raju</em>, and an object. They differ only in <strong>what fills the object slot</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n<th>What fills the object slot</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Raju likes flowers.</td>\n<td>likes</td>\n<td><em>flowers</em>: a noun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Raju knows that Sarita loves flowers.</td>\n<td>knows</td>\n<td><em>that Sarita loves flowers</em>: a whole sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Raju thinks that Sarita knows that Seema likes Mohan.</td>\n<td>thinks</td>\n<td><em>that Sarita knows that Seema likes Mohan</em>: a sentence with another sentence inside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Raju likes to eat pizza with his friends in the garden in the evening after sunset for group work.</td>\n<td>likes</td>\n<td><em>to eat pizza</em>: a sentence without tense</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In sentence 4, <em>with his friends</em>, <em>in the garden</em>, <em>in the evening</em>, <em>after sunset</em> and <em>for group work</em> are prepositional phrases working as adverbs. They give information about <em>likes</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>). These are <strong>adjuncts</strong>: their number “is not restricted”, and their order has no fixed priority as long as it makes sense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Matrix clause and embedded clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>When a sentence is placed inside a larger sentence, the inner sentence is called a <strong>clause</strong>: an <strong>embedded clause</strong> (= subordinate clause). It has no independent status outside the larger sentence.</li>\n<li>The larger sentence that holds it is the <strong>matrix clause</strong> (= main clause).</li>\n<li>So “a sentence is essentially a clause too”. The difference: a sentence has independent status, and a clause “appears dependent” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Square brackets show the embedding:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Raju knows [that Sarita loves flowers].</li>\n<li>Raju thinks [that Sarita knows [that Seema likes Mohan]]. This is “embedding within embedding”: an embedded clause inside an embedded clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">A complex sentence is a simple sentence in disguise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Each of the four sentences is just <strong>subject + verb + object</strong>. A sentence is called complex only because one of its slots holds a whole clause. That, says the lecture, is why the mind has no trouble with long sentences: it treats every complex sentence like a simple one (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>). Find the subject, the verb and what fills each slot, and no sentence is too long.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"finite-and-non-finite-clauses\">Finite and non-finite clauses</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Finite and non-finite clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>finite</strong> clause has a verb with tense and agreement: <em>Sarita <strong>loves</strong> flowers</em>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>non-finite</strong> clause has a verb with no tense and no agreement. The lecture’s examples use the <strong>infinitive</strong> (to + verb: <em>to go, to come, to get, to eat</em>), so they are also called <strong>infinitival</strong> clauses: <em>to eat pizza</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Who eats the pizza in sentence 4? Raju. The lecturer writes the hidden subject in, with a small letter under each name; the same letter means the same person (the names are <strong>co-indexed</strong>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"nw\">Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> likes <span class=\"nw\">[Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> to eat pizza]. Both names refer to the same person, so the second one is dropped: <em>Raju likes to eat pizza</em>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"nw\">Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> likes <span class=\"nw\">[Sarita<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5978em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> to eat pizza]. Different people, so the subject of the inner clause must be said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A non-finite clause cannot stand alone</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Sarita loves flowers</em> is embedded in sentence 2, but it could also stand alone as a sentence. A non-finite clause cannot: “we can never say <em>Sarita to eat pizza</em>” as a sentence. It must stay embedded, yet it is still a clause, a type of sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>). We use such clauses all the time: in <em>I want to go</em>, the object of <em>want</em> is the clause <em>to go</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-clause-can-do-in-a-sentence\">What a clause can do in a sentence</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Jobs of clauses (the summary slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Clauses can</p>\n<ul>\n<li>work like <strong>nouns</strong>, in subject, object or adjunct positions;</li>\n<li>work like an <strong>adjective</strong>, for description;</li>\n<li>work as an <strong>adverb</strong>, for modification.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Clauses as nouns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>). Each bracket could be replaced by one noun, for example <em>[A flower] is a bliss</em>, which shows that the clause works as a noun (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Job of the bracket</th>\n<th>Finite?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>[For Raju to get a good night sleep] is a bliss.</td>\n<td>subject of <em>is</em></td>\n<td>non-finite (<em>to get</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju knows [how to work on a computer].</td>\n<td>object of <em>knows</em></td>\n<td>non-finite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju wants [to go home].</td>\n<td>object of <em>wants</em>; its subject (Raju) is dropped</td>\n<td>non-finite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju wants [Sarita to go home].</td>\n<td>object of <em>wants</em>; its subject (Sarita) is said</td>\n<td>non-finite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju knows [that Sarita loves flowers].</td>\n<td>object of <em>knows</em></td>\n<td>finite</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So an object clause can be finite or non-finite (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Clauses as adjectives and adverbs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Nature</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju [who lives in Chennai] is a teacher of English.</td>\n<td>adjectival (relative clause)</td>\n<td>describes <em>Raju</em>, a noun in the subject position. <em>who</em> is a relative pronoun here, not a question word.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju is a teacher in Chennai [which is the capital of Tamil Nadu].</td>\n<td>adjectival</td>\n<td>describes <em>Chennai</em>, a noun inside an adjunct (<em>in Chennai</em>). An adjectival clause can describe a noun in any position.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>[As far as rain is concerned], it is likely to begin in July.</td>\n<td>adverbial</td>\n<td>The main clause is <em>it is likely to begin in July</em>. The bracket is not tied to the subject or to the predicate; it adds information about the whole statement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>[Given the age of the actor [who worked in many Hindi movies last year]], he looks much younger.</td>\n<td>adverbial, with an adjectival clause inside</td>\n<td>The main clause is <em>he looks much younger</em>. Inside the adverbial block, <em>who worked …</em> describes <em>actor</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>to go home</em> in <em>Raju wants to go home</em> a clause? Is it finite or non-finite, and where is its subject?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Yes, it is a clause: it has a verb (<em>go</em>) and a hidden subject. It is <strong>non-finite</strong>: <em>to go</em> has no tense and no agreement. Its subject is <em>Raju</em>, the same person as the main subject (co-indexed), so it is dropped. The whole clause is the object of <em>wants</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">How many clauses are in <em>Raju thinks that Sarita knows that Seema likes Mohan</em>, and which is the matrix clause?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Three verbs with tense (<em>thinks, knows, likes</em>), so <strong>three clauses</strong>. The whole sentence, <em>Raju thinks [ … ]</em>, is the matrix clause. <em>that Sarita knows that Seema likes Mohan</em> is embedded in it as the object of <em>thinks</em>, and <em>that Seema likes Mohan</em> is embedded again, as the object of <em>knows</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why must <em>Sarita</em> be said in <em>Raju wants Sarita to go home</em>, but no second <em>Raju</em> in <em>Raju wants to go home</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The subject of the inner clause is dropped only when it is the same person as the main subject (co-indexed: <span class=\"nw\">Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> … <span class=\"nw\">Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>).</span> In the first sentence, the one who goes is Sarita, a different person <span class=\"nw\">(Raju<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4617em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> … <span class=\"nw\">Sarita<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5978em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>),</span> so her name must appear.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"simple-compound-and-complex-in-real-texts\">Simple, compound and complex in real texts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Textbook sentences such as <em>Everyone needs a coach</em> or <em>I like fruits</em> are short. Real people speak and write long sentences: they add clauses and commas, and they even repeat parts. Yet the listener understands the speaker without any trouble. Lecture 3.3 looks at real texts to see the sentence types at work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). You do not need these terms in order to <em>speak</em>; the analysis shows how the mind connects every part of a sentence.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-speech-and-a-novel\">A speech and a novel</h3>\n<p>The first text is the opening of a talk about feedback for teachers (the 2013 TED talk by Bill Gates; the lecturer calls it “somebody’s opinion”). The lecture analyses two of its sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Everyone needs a coach.</em> A <strong>simple</strong> sentence: subject <em>Everyone</em>, verb <em>needs</em>, object <em>a coach</em>.</li>\n<li><em>We all need people who will give us feedback.</em> “A sentence technically ends” at <em>people</em>. Then <em>who will give us feedback</em> is a <strong>relative clause</strong> that modifies the noun <em>people</em>. So the sentence is complex.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The second text is one sentence from <em>Swami and Friends</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Six weeks later Rajam came to Swaminathan’s house to announce that he forgave him all his sins — starting with his political activities, to his new acquisition, the Board High School air, by which was meant a certain slowness and stupidity engendered by mental decay.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rajam’s announcement, clause by clause (the lecture’s table at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Verbs with tense (underlined on the slide): <em>came</em>, <em>forgave</em>, <em>was (meant)</em>. So three clauses.</li>\n<li>Clause 1, <strong>Six weeks later Rajam came to Swaminathan’s house to announce</strong>: the independent (matrix, main) clause. <em>came</em> is intransitive, so it needs no object; <em>to Swaminathan’s house</em> gives the place (the lecturer calls it “kind of a required adjunct”). <em>announce</em> is transitive, so it needs an object.</li>\n<li>Clause 2, <strong>that he forgave him all his sins, starting with his political activities, to his new acquisition, the Board High School air</strong>: a subordinate clause with a <strong>nominal</strong> function. It is the object of the verb <em>announce</em> (announce <strong>what</strong>?).</li>\n<li>Clause 3, <strong>by which was meant a certain slowness and stupidity engendered by mental decay</strong>: a subordinate <strong>adjectival</strong> clause. It qualifies the noun <em>air</em> in clause 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one main clause + a noun clause (object of <em>announce</em>) + an adjectival clause (qualifying <em>air</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"independent-clauses-and-simple-sentences\">Independent clauses and simple sentences</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Independent (matrix) clause and simple sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An independent clause has both a subject and a predicate (verb), so structurally it does not depend on another clause.</li>\n<li>Therefore a sentence is essentially an independent clause.</li>\n<li>All <strong>simple sentences</strong>, which means sentences with only one clause each, are independent clauses.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Long or short, these are all simple sentences: “They have only one verb and one subject each” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Water freezes at 0 degrees centigrade.</li>\n<li>Water boils at 100 degrees centigrade.</li>\n<li>The sun rises in the east.</li>\n<li>Circling the airport for a long time in the sky, the plane was finally allowed to land. (The main part, <em>the plane was finally allowed to land</em>, is passive. <em>Circling the airport …</em> has an adverbial function.)</li>\n<li>All the discoveries and inventions of the 20th century, such as from aircraft to pressure cookers, from landing on the moon to the atom bomb to personal computers, etc., have not made human beings happier. (The core is <em>All the discoveries and inventions of the 20th century have not made human beings happier</em>; the <em>such as</em> part only lists examples.)</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Every sentence has a subject and one verb with tense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Every sentence has a subject <strong>position</strong>, even when no subject is spoken. In imperatives (<em>Come here</em> = <em>(You) come here</em>) and in a non-finite clause whose subject is co-indexed (<em>Raju wants to go home</em>), the subject is dropped for a reason, and “that absence does not count as absence”.</li>\n<li>“An independent clause can be long or short, can have many other phrases, etc. But it has only one verb with tense.” Any other verb form in it is a verbal noun (a <strong>gerund</strong>, an <em>-ing</em> form used as a noun) or belongs to another clause.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"compound-sentences\">Compound sentences</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Compound sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Two or more independent clauses joined by words like <em>and</em> or <em>or</em> (also <em>but</em>), or simply by commas in a row, form a <strong>compound sentence</strong>. By definition it has more than one independent clause.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Compound sentence</th>\n<th>Independent clauses</th>\n<th>Joined by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I came, I saw, I conquered.</td>\n<td>I came / I saw / I conquered</td>\n<td>commas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sometimes I have idlis for breakfast, but some other times I also like bread.</td>\n<td>I have idlis for breakfast / I also like bread</td>\n<td>comma + <em>but</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>European workers generally go to church on Sundays or they go to work for charity on these days.</td>\n<td>European workers generally go to church on Sundays / they go to work for charity on these days</td>\n<td><em>or</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mary called the check-in desk before leaving and she reached the airport in time.</td>\n<td>Mary called the check-in desk before leaving / she reached the airport in time</td>\n<td><em>and</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>What it is made of</th>\n<th>Lecture example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simple</strong></td>\n<td>one independent clause; the whole sentence stands alone</td>\n<td>Everyone needs a coach.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compound</strong></td>\n<td>two or more independent clauses, linked by <em>and, or, but</em> or commas; each clause could stand alone</td>\n<td>I came, I saw, I conquered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Complex</strong></td>\n<td>one independent clause + one or more subordinate clauses, linked by words such as <em>that, who, which, while, when</em> (or by no word: <em>Raju wants to go home</em>); only the main clause could stand alone</td>\n<td>Raju knows that Sarita loves flowers.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Long is not the same as complex, and <em>and</em> is not always compound</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Length does not decide the type. <em>All the discoveries and inventions of the 20th century, … have not made human beings happier</em> is long but simple: one subject, one verb with tense.</li>\n<li><em>and</em> makes a compound sentence only when it joins two <strong>independent</strong> clauses. In sentence a of Lecture 3.1, <em>and (his father) admitted him there</em> is joined to the subordinate clause, so the sentence as a whole is still complex.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s Activity 1: which sentences in the Swami paragraph are single independent clauses?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The answer slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rse0Rxh1SlM&amp;t=1505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:05</a>) lists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>At first, Swaminathan was rather uncertain of his happiness in the new school.</li>\n<li>All his new class-mates, remarkably new faces, often clustered round him to see him and hear him talk.</li>\n<li>Mani saw him every day.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each has one subject and one verb with tense, and no subordinate clause. (<em>to see him and hear him talk</em> has no tense.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Simple, compound or complex? <em>Circling the airport for a long time in the sky, the plane was finally allowed to land.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Simple.</strong> There is one subject (<em>the plane</em>) and one verb with tense (<em>was allowed</em>). <em>Circling</em> has no tense, so the opening part is not a second finite clause; it has an adverbial function.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Simple, compound or complex? <em>We all need people who will give us feedback.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Complex.</strong> Main clause: <em>We all need people</em>. Subordinate clause: <em>who will give us feedback</em>, a relative clause that modifies <em>people</em>. It cannot stand alone as a sentence.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">The lecture’s Activity 2 asks whether the Swami paragraph has compound sentences. Is <em>The Pea was promoted, but he returned to the class exactly three months late, and he was quite full up with medical certificates, explanations, and exemptions</em> compound?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Yes.</strong> It has three independent clauses, each with its own subject and verb with tense: <em>The Pea was promoted</em> / <em>he returned to the class exactly three months late</em> / <em>he was quite full up with medical certificates, explanations, and exemptions</em>. They are joined by <em>but</em> and <em>and</em>. (The lecture gives no answer slide for Activity 2; this answer applies its definition.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What kind of clause is the <em>whether</em>-part in <em>It doesn’t matter whether you’re a basketball player, a tennis player, a gymnast or a bridge player</em>?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>A <strong>noun clause</strong>. Ask: <em>what</em> doesn’t matter? Whether you’re a basketball player… So the clause is the real subject, and <em>it</em> only holds its place, just like <em>It was believed … that the pandemic would disappear</em> in Lecture 3.1. (This sentence is from the speech in Lecture 3.3, which does not analyse it; this answer applies the 3.1 rule.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"the-adjectival-relative-clause\">The adjectival (relative) clause</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Why do people use long sentences at all? Lecture 3.4 gives a scientific reason. For the human mind, “a sentence is a sentence”: long or short makes no difference to processing, because the mind checks only grammar. And a real conversation or text (a <strong>discourse</strong>) needs larger sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). One common way to make a sentence larger is to describe a noun with a whole clause. That clause is the <strong>adjectival clause</strong>, also called the <strong>relative clause</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A quick recap from the start of the lecture (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Subject and predicate are connected by <strong>agreement</strong>. In English it works with two features, <strong>number</strong> and <strong>person</strong>. Sometimes it is visible (<em>likes</em>), sometimes not.</li>\n<li>A <strong>compound</strong> sentence joins two independent sentences with a comma or with connectors like <em>and</em>, <em>or</em>. A <strong>complex</strong> sentence has at least one subordinate clause with its main clause.</li>\n<li><strong>Nominal</strong> clauses stand in a subject or object position (and sometimes in apposition). <strong>Adjectival</strong> clauses modify a noun. <strong>Adverbial</strong> clauses give additional information, about a noun, about the whole predicate or about something else: “as long as it is an adjunct, it is adverbial in nature” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjectival clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A clause that can stand for a simple adjective. It has three features:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It works like an adjective: it modifies a noun, and that noun can be in any position (subject, object or other).</li>\n<li>It can be replaced by an adjective.</li>\n<li>It always comes to the <strong>right</strong> of the noun (after it). An ordinary adjective comes to the <strong>left</strong> of the noun (before it).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Relative pronoun (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Most adjectival clauses begin with a <strong>wh-word</strong> such as <em>which</em>, <em>who</em>, <em>where</em> or <em>when</em>, or with <em>that</em>. At the start of a subordinate clause, such a word is not a question word: it is a <strong>relative pronoun</strong>. It is called a pronoun because it refers to another noun, the noun that the clause modifies. These words have two jobs: sometimes they ask questions, sometimes they begin relative clauses.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">His “room” (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>By eight he was at his desk in his ‘room’, which was only a corner in his father’s dressing-room.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Main (independent) clause: <strong>By eight he was at his desk in his room</strong>. <em>By eight</em> is an adjunct (it gives the time).</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause: <strong>which was only a corner in his father’s dressing-room</strong>. It has a verb with tense (<em>was</em>), and it begins with the wh-word <em>which</em>.</li>\n<li>Which noun does it describe? <em>room</em>. It tells us what kind of room it was: only a corner of a bigger room. That is why <em>room</em> is in quotation marks.</li>\n<li>Replace the clause with one adjective: <em>By eight he was at his desk in his <strong>corner</strong> room.</em> The adjective <em>corner</em> goes before the noun; the clause went after it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>which was only a corner in his father’s dressing-room</em> is a subordinate adjectival clause qualifying <em>room</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjectives go left, adjectival clauses go right</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Adjectives precede the noun; adjectival clauses follow the noun” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <strong>corner</strong> room (adjective, before)</li>\n<li>a room <strong>which was only a corner</strong> (clause, after)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The clause can also be replaced by a <strong>prepositional phrase</strong> such as <em>on the roof</em>, <em>behind the wall</em>, <em>beside the entrance</em> or <em>by the entrance</em>: <em>his room by the entrance</em>. All these phrases work like an adjective qualifying <em>room</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A phrase after a noun is not a clause, and not an adjective</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>by the entrance</em> has no verb, no tense and no subject, so it is a phrase, not a clause. It works <strong>like</strong> an adjective because it qualifies the noun, but the lecture is clear: “They are not adjectives; they are adjuncts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"one-main-clause-two-different-subordinate-clauses\">One main clause, two different subordinate clauses</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two clauses with two jobs (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums, Swaminathan was gazing on his face, which seemed so tame at close quarters.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Main clause: <strong>Swaminathan was gazing on his face</strong>.</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause i: <strong>While the teacher was scrutinising the sums</strong>. It only gives additional information (when?), so it is <strong>adverbial</strong>.</li>\n<li>Subordinate clause ii: <strong>which seemed so tame at close quarters</strong>. It describes the noun <em>face</em>, so it is <strong>adjectival</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> one independent clause can carry several subordinate clauses with different functions: here one adverbial and one adjectival (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two more of the lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Main clause</th>\n<th>Adjectival clause → noun it qualifies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>It was taken by D. Pillai, who had earned a name in the school for kindness and good humour.</td>\n<td>It was taken by D. Pillai</td>\n<td><em>who had earned a name in the school for kindness and good humour</em> → <em>D. Pillai</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>There were moments in it that brought stirring pictures before one.</td>\n<td>There were moments in it</td>\n<td><em>that brought stirring pictures before one</em> → <em>moments</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>that</em> has more than one job</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>In <em>that book</em>, <em>that</em> points at something (a demonstrative pronoun).</li>\n<li>In <em>the four <strong>that</strong> he liked</em>, it is a <strong>relative pronoun</strong>: it begins an adjectival clause and refers back to the noun <em>four</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li>In <em>Raju knows <strong>that</strong> Sarita loves flowers</em>, it begins a <strong>noun clause</strong> (knows <strong>what</strong>?).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A quick test (mine, not the lecture’s): if you can swap <em>that</em> for <em>who</em> or <em>which</em>, it begins an adjectival clause. <em>all strangers who came his way</em> works; ✗ <em>Raju knows which Sarita loves flowers</em> does not.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-activity\">The lecture’s activity</h3>\n<p>The lecture gives a paragraph from <em>Swami and Friends</em> and asks you to find the sentences with a subordinate adjectival clause, and the noun each one qualifies (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>). Try each sentence before you open the answer.</p>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Those were the four that he liked and admired most in his class.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Main clause: <strong>Those were the four</strong>. Adjectival clause: <strong>that he liked and admired most in his class</strong>, qualifying the noun <strong>four</strong>. Here <em>that</em> is a relative pronoun. (Answer slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>The first was Somu, the Monitor, who carried himself with such an easy air.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Main clause: <strong>The first was Somu, the Monitor</strong>. Adjectival clause: <strong>who carried himself with such an easy air</strong>, qualifying <strong>Somu, the monitor</strong>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>He set about his business, whatever it was, with absolute confidence and calmness.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Main clause: <strong>He set about his business</strong>. Adjectival clause: <strong>whatever it was</strong>, qualifying the noun <strong>business</strong>. The slide’s box also includes <em>with absolute confidence and calmness</em>; the lecturer brackets it separately as a prepositional phrase that, he says, qualifies the same noun (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>It was said that a new teacher who once tried it very nearly lost his life.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Three parts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EK2zwv9WO1A&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Main clause: <strong>It was said</strong> (passive).</li>\n<li>Subordinate <strong>noun</strong> clause: <strong>that a new teacher … very nearly lost his life</strong> (said <strong>what</strong>?).</li>\n<li>Subordinate <strong>adjectival</strong> clause inside it: <strong>who once tried it</strong>, qualifying <strong>teacher</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Mani bullied all strangers that came his way, be they big or small.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Main clause: <strong>Mani bullied all strangers</strong>. Adjectival clause: <strong>that came his way, be they big or small</strong>, qualifying <strong>strangers</strong>. Again <em>that</em> is a relative pronoun.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Replace the adjectival clause with one adjective: <em>He lived in a room which was only a corner.</em> (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>He lived in a <strong>corner</strong> room.</em> The adjective moves to the left of the noun; the clause stood on its right.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"complements-and-adjuncts\">Complements and adjuncts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Accuracy in English means grammatical sentences, and fluency means sentences that flow in speech. Both start with knowing what a sentence <strong>must</strong> contain (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Think of an online form: some fields are <strong>required</strong> (marked with a star) and some are <strong>optional</strong>. Leave a required field empty and the form is rejected; leave an optional one empty and it still goes through. In a sentence, the required parts are <strong>complements</strong> and the optional parts are <strong>adjuncts</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verbs decide their objects (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>transitive</strong> verb takes one object: <em>John loves <strong>Mary</strong>.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>ditransitive</strong> verb takes two objects. (The lecture gives no example. Mine: <em>Raju gave <strong>Sarita</strong> <strong>a book</strong>.</em>)</li>\n<li>An <strong>intransitive</strong> verb takes no object: <em>Sit down.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because the verb decides whether objects appear, objects are part of the verb: verb + object form one <strong>verb phrase</strong>. So <strong>objects are complements</strong>. Adverbs also describe the verb, but the verb’s type does not decide whether they appear, so <strong>adverbs are adjuncts</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Complement and adjunct (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>complement</strong> is an essential, structurally <strong>indispensable</strong> part of a sentence, clause or phrase. If you remove it, the sentence becomes incomplete: ungrammatical.</li>\n<li>An <strong>adjunct</strong> is an optional, structurally <strong>dispensable</strong> part of a sentence, clause or phrase. If you remove it, the structure of the sentence is not affected (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The complement slide has a misprint, “(structurally dispensable)”; the lecturer corrects it in ink to <strong>indispensable</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"word-order-and-agreement\">Word order and agreement</h3>\n<p>All sentences, in every language, have a subject and a predicate. The order inside the predicate differs (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, Bengali, Assamese, Marathi, Hindi</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Order</td>\n<td>subject + verb + object</td>\n<td>subject + object + verb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Name</td>\n<td><strong>verb-medial</strong> (verb in the middle)</td>\n<td><strong>verb-final</strong> (verb at the end)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pattern</td>\n<td><em>John loves Mary.</em></td>\n<td><em>John Mary loves</em> (word for word)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>This order inside the predicate is a major structural difference between our languages and English.</p>\n<p>Subject and verb are not two separate parts: they are bound by <strong>agreement</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). Agreement has three parts: number, person and gender; in English, gender shows up only in a very limited way.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Subject: number, person</th>\n<th>Agreement mark on the verb</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>John loves Mary.</td>\n<td><em>John</em>: singular, third person</td>\n<td><em>loves</em>: the <em>-s</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>John likes pizza with his friends.</td>\n<td><em>John</em>: singular, third person</td>\n<td><em>likes</em>: the <em>-s</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>John and Mary like pizza in the evening.</td>\n<td><em>John and Mary</em>: plural, third person</td>\n<td><em>like</em>: zero (nothing) marks the plural</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject\">Imperatives have a hidden subject</h3>\n<p>An <strong>imperative</strong> sentence gives an order or makes a request (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(You) come here.</em> <em>come</em> is intransitive; <em>here</em> is an adverb, an adjunct of place.</li>\n<li><em>(You) sit down.</em> Intransitive, with no object and no adjunct.</li>\n<li><em>(You) drink a glass of water before food.</em> <em>drink</em> is transitive; <em>a glass of water</em> is the object, a complement; <em>before food</em> is an adjunct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The subject of every imperative is <em>you</em>. Because it is always the same, the mind does not say it: “when it is obvious, it must not be articulated”. This is the <strong>principle of economy</strong>: the absence of the subject still registers its presence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\"><em>drink</em> without an object</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>drink</em> is a special verb. When its object is left out, it means alcohol: <em>Do not drink</em> means do not drink alcohol, as in <em>Do not drink and drive</em>. To mean anything else, you must say what (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"testing-for-complements-take-it-out\">Testing for complements: take it out</h3>\n<p>Each sentence below has lost its object, and each one leaves the question “<strong>what?</strong>” open (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>What is missing</th>\n<th>Complete version</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ Raju needs (what?) for his exam.</td>\n<td>the object of <em>needs</em></td>\n<td>Raju needs <strong>a pen</strong> for his exam.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ Ramu eats (what?) after dinner.</td>\n<td>the object of <em>eats</em></td>\n<td>Ramu eats <strong>an apple</strong> after dinner.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✗ Seema reads (what?) in the morning.</td>\n<td>the object of <em>reads</em></td>\n<td>Seema reads <strong>a novel</strong> in the morning.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The complete versions use fillers suggested in the lecture. In <em>Raju needs a pen for his exam</em>, <em>for his exam</em> is an adjunct: <em>Raju needs a pen</em> is already complete.</p>\n<h3 id=\"testing-for-adjuncts-take-it-out\">Testing for adjuncts: take it out</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>✓ <em>Raju helped Ramu in the morning.</em> Take out <em>in the morning</em> and <em>Raju helped Ramu</em> is still complete, so it is an adjunct. It gives the time of the helping, so it modifies the verb <em>helped</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li>✗ <em>Raju helped (whom?) in the morning after breakfast.</em> Two adjuncts of time are present, but the complement is missing, so the slide marks it as wrong (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Following the lecture</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In everyday English some of these verbs can be used without an object (<em>She reads a lot</em>). For this course, follow the lecture: in these sentences the question “what?” is left open, so the object slot is empty and the sentence is incomplete.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"an-adjunct-of-the-verb-or-of-a-noun\">An adjunct of the verb or of a noun?</h3>\n<p>An adjunct can belong to the verb or to a noun phrase inside the predicate. The lecture first asks you to decide by intuition, then gives its answers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>Adjunct</th>\n<th>It is an adjunct of</th>\n<th>The lecture’s words</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>John likes pizza with his friends.</td>\n<td><em>with his friends</em></td>\n<td>the noun phrase <em>pizza</em></td>\n<td>“this whole PP <em>with his friends</em> is adjunct of this noun phrase, <em>pizza</em>”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>John and Mary like pizza in the evening.</td>\n<td><em>in the evening</em></td>\n<td>the verb <em>like</em></td>\n<td>“<em>likes in the evening</em> is about liking”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju drinks a glass of water before bed.</td>\n<td><em>before bed</em></td>\n<td>the noun phrase <em>water</em></td>\n<td>“What does he drink? Water before bed.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raju helped Ramu in the morning.</td>\n<td><em>in the morning</em></td>\n<td>the verb <em>helped</em></td>\n<td>“it is modifying the verb”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">“Adj” on the 3.5 slides means adjunct</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Lecture 3.5 the lecturer’s ink label “Adj” (next to <em>with his friends</em>, <em>before food</em>, <em>in the morning</em>) means <strong>adjunct</strong>, not adjective. Complements are marked “Comp”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"complements-and-adjuncts-inside-a-phrase\">Complements and adjuncts inside a phrase</h3>\n<p>Not only sentences: a <strong>phrase</strong> can have a complement and adjuncts too (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a>). The <strong>head</strong> of a phrase is its main word, the one the other words depend on: <em>king</em> in <em>the King of England</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phrase</th>\n<th>OK?</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the King of England</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td><em>of England</em> is the complement of <em>king</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the King of England with his ministers</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>complement first, then the adjunct <em>with his ministers</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the King with his ministers of England</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>the adjunct has come between the head and its complement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a student of physics</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td><em>of physics</em> is the complement of <em>student</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a student with long hair</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td><em>with long hair</em> is an adjunct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a student of physics with long hair</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n<td>complement, then adjunct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a student with long hair of physics</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>adjunct between head and complement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the King of England of France</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>two complements for one head</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a student of physics of English</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n<td>two complements for one head</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two rules (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Position</strong>: complements stay close to their head; adjuncts stay away from it. An adjunct between a head and its complement makes the phrase ungrammatical.</li>\n<li><strong>Number</strong>: one complement in a phrase, and at most two complements in a sentence (a verb can take two objects). Many adjuncts are possible in a phrase or a sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=2370s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Complement</th>\n<th>Adjunct</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Required?</td>\n<td>yes: structurally indispensable</td>\n<td>no: structurally dispensable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If you remove it</td>\n<td>incomplete, ungrammatical</td>\n<td>still complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who decides if it appears?</td>\n<td>the head (the verb’s type: transitive or not)</td>\n<td>not the head</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical form</td>\n<td>object of a verb; <em>of</em>-phrase after a noun</td>\n<td>adverb; prepositional phrase of time, place, company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How many?</td>\n<td>one per phrase; at most two per sentence</td>\n<td>as many as you like</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Position</td>\n<td>next to the head</td>\n<td>after the complement, further away</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture example</td>\n<td>John loves <strong>Mary</strong>; the King <strong>of England</strong></td>\n<td>Raju helped Ramu <strong>in the morning</strong>; a student <strong>with long hair</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Four quick tests</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What-test</strong>: ask “verb + what?”. The answer is the object, a complement (<em>needs <strong>what</strong>? a pen</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Delete test</strong>: remove the part. If the rest breaks, it was a complement; if the rest is fine, it was an adjunct.</li>\n<li><strong>Swap test</strong> (in a phrase): put it after the other phrase. A complement cannot move away from its head: ✗ <em>a student with long hair of physics</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Count test</strong>: a second <em>of</em>-phrase (✗ <em>of England of France</em>) cannot be a second complement.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecturer’s favourite exercise</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Take twenty sentences. Underline the subject and the predicate. Inside each predicate find the verb. Then mark the complements of the verb, and the phrases that are adjuncts of the verb or of a noun phrase (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3nAOsJgIuDo&amp;t=2520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the bold part a complement or an adjunct? <em>John loves <strong>Mary</strong>.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Complement.</strong> <em>loves</em> is transitive and needs an object: loves <strong>whom</strong>? Remove <em>Mary</em> and <em>John loves</em> is incomplete.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the bold part a complement or an adjunct? <em>Raju helped Ramu <strong>in the morning</strong>.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Adjunct</strong>, of the verb <em>helped</em>. Remove it and <em>Raju helped Ramu</em> is still complete; it only adds the time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the bold part a complement or an adjunct? <em>(You) drink <strong>a glass of water</strong> before food.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Complement.</strong> <em>drink</em> is transitive here; <em>a glass of water</em> is its object. (<em>before food</em> is the adjunct.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the bold part a complement or an adjunct? <em>a student of physics <strong>with long hair</strong></em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Adjunct</strong>, of the noun <em>student</em>. It comes after the complement (<em>of physics</em>), and it could be left out: <em>a student of physics</em> is a complete phrase.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Why is <em>the King with his ministers of England</em> wrong?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>of England</em> is the complement of <em>king</em>, and a complement must stay next to its head. Here the adjunct <em>with his ministers</em> has come between them. Correct: <em>the King of England with his ministers</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clause</strong>: own subject + verb with tense. <strong>Independent</strong> (main, matrix) clause: can stand alone. <strong>Subordinate</strong> (embedded, dependent) clause: depends on a word in the main clause.</li>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong> = one independent clause. <strong>Compound</strong> = two or more independent clauses joined by <em>and, or, but</em> or commas. <strong>Complex</strong> = an independent clause + one or more subordinate clauses. Length does not decide the type.</li>\n<li><strong>Noun clause</strong>: subject, object, or in apposition. <strong>Adjectival clause</strong>: qualifies a noun. <strong>Adverbial clause</strong>: adds information.</li>\n<li><strong>Clause analysis</strong>: count the clauses → nature (noun, adjectival, adverbial) → function (x qualifies y; subject or object of z; adds information).</li>\n<li><strong>Finite</strong> clause: verb with tense; can stand alone. <strong>Non-finite</strong> clause (<em>to go</em>): no tense or agreement; must stay embedded. A co-indexed subject is dropped (<em>Raju wants to go home</em>); a different subject is said (<em>Raju wants Sarita to go home</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Adjectival (relative) clause</strong>: begins with a relative pronoun (<em>who, which, that</em>, also <em>where, when</em>), follows its noun, can be replaced by an adjective (which goes before the noun). A prepositional phrase after a noun works like an adjective but is an adjunct, not a clause.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentence</strong> = subject + predicate; <strong>predicate</strong> = verb + object(s) + adjuncts. Subject and verb <strong>agree</strong> in number and person (<em>loves</em>; <em>like</em> with a zero mark for the plural). English is <strong>verb-medial</strong>; many Indian languages are <strong>verb-final</strong>. Imperatives have the hidden subject <em>you</em> (principle of economy).</li>\n<li><strong>Complement</strong>: required (objects; <em>of England</em>); remove it and the sentence breaks; next to its head; one per phrase, at most two per sentence. <strong>Adjunct</strong>: optional (adverbs, most PPs); remove it and nothing breaks; further from the head; as many as you like.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Simple, compound or complex? <em>Mary called the check-in desk before leaving and she reached the airport in time.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Compound.</strong> Two independent clauses, each with its own subject and verb with tense (<em>Mary called …</em>, <em>she reached …</em>), joined by <em>and</em>. <em>before leaving</em> has no tense, so it is not a third clause.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find the main clause and the subordinate clause, and name the subordinate clause’s kind: <em>The book that you lent me is on the table.</em> (my sentence)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Main clause: <strong>The book is on the table</strong>. Subordinate clause: <strong>that you lent me</strong>. It is <strong>adjectival</strong>: it comes right after the noun <em>book</em> and tells us <em>which</em> book; <em>that</em> is a relative pronoun (you could say <em>which you lent me</em>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is <em>to go</em> in <em>I want to go</em> a clause? What job does it do?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Yes: a <strong>non-finite</strong> (infinitival) clause. It is the <strong>object</strong> of <em>want</em> (want <strong>what</strong>?). Its subject is <em>I</em>, the same person as the main subject, so it is dropped: <em>I want [I to go]</em> becomes <em>I want to go</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is the subject of <em>Sit down</em>? Does the sentence have an object?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The subject is <em><strong>you</strong></em>, present but not spoken: in every imperative the subject is <em>you</em>, so the mind leaves it out (principle of economy). There is no object, because <em>sit (down)</em> is intransitive, and there is no adjunct either.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Find all the clauses and name each one: <em>Given the age of the actor who worked in many Hindi movies last year, he looks much younger.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>Main clause: <strong>he looks much younger</strong>.</li>\n<li>Adverbial block: <strong>Given the age of the actor …</strong>: it adds information to the whole sentence.</li>\n<li>Inside it, an adjectival clause: <strong>who worked in many Hindi movies last year</strong>, describing <em>actor</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A clause can sit inside another subordinate part (Lecture 3.2, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oJP60we1YUY&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Is the bold part a complement or an adjunct? <em>the King <strong>of England</strong> with his ministers</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Complement</strong> of <em>king</em>. It stands right next to its head, and there can be only one such <em>of</em>-phrase: ✗ <em>the King of England of France</em>. <em>with his ministers</em> is the adjunct.</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
